15
00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:31.454
<v Maya>That is a massive, incredibly diverse data set. So when they zoomed out and looked at all these people working alongside AI, what was the bottom line? Like, did the suit work?</v>

16
00:02:31.554 --> 00:02:39.918
<v Daniel>The headline was pretty bleak, honestly. On average, human-AI combinations did worse than the better of the human or the AI acting entirely alone.</v>

17
00:02:40.018 --> 00:02:40.678
<v Maya>Wait, worse?</v>

18
00:02:41.078 --> 00:02:45.581
<v Daniel>Yeah, worse. In statistical terms, they found a Hedges G of about negative 0.23.</v>

19
00:02:46.695 --> 00:03:00.862
<v Maya>OK, let me stop you there for a second, just so we don't lose anyone in the math. Hedges G is essentially a way of standardizing performance differences across totally different types of experiments, right? So you can actually compare apples to oranges, like baggage screening versus medical scans.</v>

20
00:03:01.422 --> 00:03:13.188
<v Daniel>Exactly. It gives us a standardized effect size, so everything is on the same scale. And a negative 0.23 is significant. So the prevailing narrative in the scientific community just became humans plus AI underperform.</v>

21
00:03:13.308 --> 00:03:13.968
<v Maya>That's wild.</v>

22
00:03:14.457 --> 00:03:29.007
<v Daniel>Now, Vaccaro and colleagues did find some nuances. Like, there were performance losses on decision-making tasks, but some gains on creative, generative tasks. But the real shocker to many researchers was that giving the AI the ability to express its</v>

23
00:03:29.067 --> 00:03:36.252
<v Daniel>confidence level, or providing the human with an explanation of how the AI reached its conclusion, it didn't actually help overall.</v>

24
00:03:36.412 --> 00:03:51.300
<v Maya>Wait, negative 0.23? So it's not just that the AI isn't helping, it's that the combination is actively dragging down the top performer. Yes. It's like pairing a Michelin star master chef with a high-tech recipe bot. You'd think they would create culinary</v>

25
00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:58.804
<v Maya>magic, but somehow the final dish tastes worse than if the chef just cooked from memory or the robot just, you know, followed its programming alone.</v>

26
00:03:59.044 --> 00:04:04.987
<v Daniel>That is a brilliant way to visualize it. And you're right, the fact that explanations didn't help was a massive blow to the industry.</v>

27
00:04:05.302 --> 00:04:18.412
<v Maya>Which is wild to me because we're constantly told that explainable AI is the holy grail. Like if the machine can just tell you why it thinks a patient has a disease or why a loan should be denied, surely the human can make a better call.</v>

28
00:04:18.472 --> 00:04:19.613
<v Daniel>Right, that's the intuition. But</v>

29
00:04:19.653 --> 00:04:29.800
<v Maya>the data said no. So, I mean, are we just inherently bad at collaborating with machines? Are human brains just too stubborn or too easily confused by algorithms?</v>

30
00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:35.223
