36
00:05:01.991 --> 00:05:15.202
<v Daniel>Well, they went back and recoded every single one of those 74 studies to look for one missing variable, which is human learning. And they found some shocking flaws in how these lab environments were set up.</v>

37
00:05:15.402 --> 00:05:15.883
<v Maya>Like what?</v>

38
00:05:16.123 --> 00:05:21.027
<v Daniel>The typical study they reviewed was just a single session. Participants were given a median of only 30 trials.</v>

39
00:05:22.383 --> 00:05:23.083
<v Maya>That's nothing.</v>

40
00:05:23.143 --> 00:05:36.971
<v Daniel>It's nothing. There were zero practice trials to get used to the AI's quirks. But, and here is the most damning part, out of the 74 studies, only 10 ever actually told the participants what the right answer was after they made a decision.</v>

41
00:05:37.091 --> 00:05:38.031
<v Maya>Wait, seriously.</v>

42
00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:38.692
<v Daniel>Seriously.</v>

43
00:05:38.732 --> 00:05:45.861
<v Maya>Those scientists were testing how well humans use AI. But keeping the humans completely in the dark about whether the AI was actually right or wrong.</v>

44
00:05:45.921 --> 00:05:52.824
<v Daniel>Yes. Out of 370 distinct effect sizes, only 98 featured any outcome feedback at all.</v>

45
00:05:53.005 --> 00:05:54.465
<v Maya>That is crazy.</v>

46
00:05:54.545 --> 00:06:09.273
<v Daniel>And none of them varied the feedback experimentally. Put yourself in the shoes of a participant for a second. You're staring at a screen. You're asked to classify a lung scan. The AI pops up and says, I think it's a humor. You click agree or disagree. And then the screen just flashes to the next</v>

47
00:06:09.293 --> 00:06:16.075
<v Daniel>scan. You never find out if it actually was a tumor. You never find out if the A.I. just gave you brilliant advice or terrible advice.</v>

48
00:06:16.556 --> 00:06:26.680
<v Maya>How on earth is someone supposed to build an accurate mental model of an A.I. teammate if they never know when it's lying to them? I mean, if you don't know the outcome, you are just clicking buttons in a vacuum.</v>

49
00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:38.124
<v Daniel>Exactly. You can't build that mental model. And this isn't an isolated critique either. We can look at a parallel critique from a 2025 paper by Holman, Cale and Hartline to see how widespread this is.</v>

50
00:06:38.465 --> 00:06:39.265
<v Maya>OK, what did they find?</v>

51
00:06:39.677 --> 00:06:51.602
<v Daniel>they analyzed a completely different set of AI assisted decision experiments. And they found that out of 39 studies, only 10 gave participants enough information to even identify the correct answer.</v>

52
00:06:51.802 --> 00:07:05.227
<v Maya>Wow. So these two critiques combined are basically saying the entire scientific field might be drawing these sweeping conclusions about human bias and human failure from like the absolute worst possible corner of the experimental design space. Precisely.</v>

53
00:07:05.838 --> 00:07:13.767
<v Daniel>I mean, if you put someone in a vacuum, you don't let them practice and you actively hide from them when they make a mistake. Of course, they're going to underperform.</v>

54
00:07:13.847 --> 00:07:27.294
