55
00:07:27.574 --> 00:07:27.914
<v Daniel>They did.</v>

56
00:07:28.414 --> 00:07:39.339
<v Maya>But wait, I have to ask. Just telling someone good job or bad job at the end of a task, that can't possibly be enough to reverse the entire AI penalty, can it? What do the actual numbers look like?</v>

57
00:07:39.591 --> 00:07:54.419
<v Daniel>Well, the numbers are fascinating. Berger's team used what are called robust Bayesian meta-regressions, which is really just a highly rigorous statistical method to analyze the data. Now, we do need to be intellectually honest with you here because the</v>

58
00:07:54.459 --> 00:07:59.482
<v Daniel>sample size of studies that actually included feedback is so small, these results are tentative.</v>

59
00:07:59.642 --> 00:08:00.482
<v Maya>Okay, fair enough. But they</v>

60
00:08:00.522 --> 00:08:02.063
<v Daniel>are incredibly compelling.</v>

61
00:08:02.143 --> 00:08:05.145
<v Maya>Here's where it gets really interesting. Let's hear the breakdown.</v>

62
00:08:05.413 --> 00:08:13.402
<v Daniel>When humans were given no feedback, the human AI synergy trended negative, a Hedges G of about negative 0.17. Okay,</v>

63
00:08:13.422 --> 00:08:14.903
<v Maya>that matches the gloomy baseline.</v>

64
00:08:14.943 --> 00:08:22.011
<v Daniel>Right. But when participants did receive outcome feedback, the synergy shifted. It trended positive to about 0.12.</v>

65
00:08:23.393 --> 00:08:35.537
<v Maya>Oh, wow. So we aren't just moving from failing to neutral. We are moving from actively sabotaging the AI to actually achieving that one plus one equals three promise we talked about in the intro.</v>

66
00:08:35.638 --> 00:08:45.001
<v Daniel>Exactly. That is a contrast about 0.34. And their statistical models show an 84 percent probability of that shift being in a positive direction.</v>

67
00:08:45.268 --> 00:08:46.429
<v Maya>That's a huge swing.</v>

68
00:08:46.689 --> 00:08:54.496
<v Daniel>It is. But the sharpest, most crucial finding, what I would call the real aha moment of this deep dive, has to be with those AI explanations we mentioned earlier.</v>

69
00:08:54.696 --> 00:08:59.700
<v Maya>Right. The explainable AI that Vaccaro's massive meta-analysis said was essentially useless.</v>

70
00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:06.005
<v Daniel>Right. Well, Berger found that giving a human an AI explanation without any outcome feedback is actually actively harmful.</v>

71
00:09:06.105 --> 00:09:06.746
<v Maya>Harmful.</v>

72
00:09:06.786 --> 00:09:11.650
<v Daniel>Yes. The synergy plummets to a negative 0.31 with a Bayes factor of 17.</v>

73
00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:17.973
<v Maya>Whoa, hang on. A Bayes factor of 17. Let's translate that from Satspeak for a second.</v>

74
00:09:17.993 --> 00:09:30.338
<v Daniel>Sure. In plain English, a Bayes factor of 17 basically means the evidence for this finding is overwhelmingly strong. It's not a statistical fluke. Explanations without feedback cause real damage.</v>

75
