<v Maya>Okay, but what if they do have feedback?</v>

76
00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:47.609
<v Daniel>If you give the human an AI explanation and you pair it with outcome feedback, the synergy skyrockets. It tends positive to about 0.3. That is a massive contrast of about 0.6 compared to the no feedback condition.</v>

77
00:09:47.749 --> 00:10:02.642
<v Maya>OK, let me make sure I'm wrapping my head around this. An AI explanation without feedback is basically like a highly charismatic, fast talking car salesperson. I like that. Right. Like they can talk you into buying an absolute lemon of a car because their logic sounds so flawless and</v>

78
00:10:02.682 --> 00:10:09.528
<v Maya>confident in the moment. And because you never get to see the car facts to check if they're telling the truth, you just blindly trust their convincing story.</v>

79
00:10:09.863 --> 00:10:24.654
<v Daniel>That is a perfect analogy. Explanations make the AI persuasive, but persuasion has nothing to do with accuracy. The explanation only helps if you have the feedback loop, the Carfax, as you said, to learn how to verify the AI's work over time. And Berger's team actually</v>

80
00:10:24.694 --> 00:10:26.095
<v Daniel>proved this within the studies, too. Oh,</v>

81
00:10:26.115 --> 00:10:27.076
<v Maya>they did? How?</v>

82
00:10:27.096 --> 00:10:41.885
<v Daniel>They looked deeply into three specific experiments, what they called trial-level data sets, and watched it happen in real time. People actually learned to lean on a better performing A.I. over time, file by trial, but only when those informative feedback signals were</v>

