95
00:11:58.099 --> 00:12:12.504
<v Daniel>Yeah. When a machine gives us a highly detailed, logical-sounding paragraph... our brains take a cognitive shortcut. We subconsciously decide, well, the computer did the heavy lifting, it sounds authoritative, so I don't need to burn precious mental calories double checking its logic.</v>

96
00:12:12.904 --> 00:12:27.869
<v Maya>Which completely changes how we should look at human training. I mean, if humans are this easily manipulated by a well-written AI explanation, even when they do get feedback, should we even be trying to teach humans to calibrate their trust? It sounds like we</v>

97
00:12:27.929 --> 00:12:30.690
<v Maya>are just hardwired to fall for a confident answer.</v>

98
00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:43.514
<v Daniel>That is the exact debate happening in the field right now. Is it a training problem or a design problem? Interestingly, Berger and his colleagues published a second paper in 2026 offering a totally different solution to this.</v>

99
00:12:43.794 --> 00:12:44.335
<v Maya>Oh, really?</v>

100
00:12:44.495 --> 00:12:51.257
<v Daniel>Yeah. They argued that if training humans to calibrate their trust is too difficult, maybe we need to change the workflow itself.</v>

101
00:12:51.457 --> 00:12:55.078
<v Maya>Okay. How do we change the workflow to protect us from our own lazy brains?</v>

102
00:12:55.548 --> 00:13:09.199
<v Daniel>It's called the hybrid confirmation tree, or HCT. Think about how almost all the studies we've talked about are set up. The AI acts as an advisor. You look at a problem. The AI withsters its advice in your ear along with its reasoning, and then you make the final call.</v>

103
00:13:09.339 --> 00:13:10.400
<v Maya>Right, the standard setup.</v>

104
00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:13.483
<v Daniel>The HCT throws that entire model out the window.</v>

105
00:13:13.543 --> 00:13:16.746
<v Maya>So no whispering in the ear. How does a decision get made?</v>

106
00:13:17.086 --> 00:13:25.744
<v Daniel>In the HCT model, the human, the AI, judge the problem completely independently. You don't see the AI's answer, and the AI doesn't see yours.</v>

107
00:13:26.124 --> 00:13:26.845
<v Maya>Oh, interesting.</v>

108
00:13:26.925 --> 00:13:33.329
<v Daniel>If you both agree, that's the final decision. If you disagree, the problem gets kicked to a second human to break the tie.</v>

109
00:13:33.829 --> 00:13:43.035
<v Maya>That is fascinating. It completely removes the psychological influence of the charismatic AI. Like, you can't be persuaded by a fast-talking salesperson if you never actually meet the salesperson.</v>

110
00:13:43.095 --> 00:13:52.241
<v Daniel>Exactly. And the results were stunning. The HCT model beat the traditional AI of a visor setup on all 10 datasets they tested it on.</v>

111
00:13:52.561 --> 00:13:52.982
<v Maya>All 10.</v>

112
00:13:53.082 --> 00:14:07.821
<v Daniel>All 10. From detecting skin cancer to predicting recidivism to spotting deep fakes, it outperformed across all levels of human expertise. Wow. Why? Because the data shows that humans simply struggle to tell correct from incorrect AI advice when they are exposed to it</v>

113
00:14:07.861 --> 00:14:08.302
<v Daniel>directly.</v>

114
00:14:08.626 --> 00:14:23.422
<v Maya>So what does this all mean? We have a real tension here. Is the future of AI synergy about building better interfaces to train humans to be better lie detectors using feedback loops like the PNAS paper suggests? Or is the future about</v>

115
00:14:23.442 --> 00:14:30.850
<v Maya>designing defensive workflows like the HCT where humans never even see the AI's advice in the first place because we frankly just can't handle it?</v>

116
00:14:31.232 --> 00:14:46.089
<v Daniel>it is likely going to depend heavily on the environment you are operating in. And that brings us to the real-world caveats of Berger's PNAS paper. We need to remember that their meta regression was correlational. It was between study and it was ultimately based on only 24</v>

117
00:14:46.169 --> 00:14:50.713
<v Daniel>effect sizes that actually featured both AI explanations and feedback.</v>

118
00:14:51.033 --> 00:14:52.574
<v Maya>Right. A relatively small slice of the pie.</v>

119
00:14:52.614 --> 00:15:00.280
<v Daniel>Very small. But the biggest caveat comes down to a concept by the psychologist Robin Hogarth. It's the difference between kind and wicked learning environments.</v>

120
00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:04.483
<v Maya>OK, I love this concept. Walk me through what makes a learning environment kind or wicked.</v>

121
00:15:04.915 --> 00:15:19.618
<v Daniel>A kind learning environment is one where feedback is accurate, it is timely, and the features you need to pay attention to are obvious. In these environments, you can learn very quickly. Okay. A wicked learning environment is one where feedback is noisy, it's delayed by months or</v>

122
00:15:19.678 --> 00:15:31.540
<v Daniel>years, or the connection between your decision and the outcome is incredibly murky. Feedback only creates human AI synergy if the environment is kind enough to let you learn from it in the first place.</v>

123
00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:46.707
<v Maya>So a kind environment is like playing a video game. You're running through a level. You jump too late. You fall into a pit and lose a life instantly. Boom. Perfect, clean, immediate feedback. You adjust your timing on the next try. Exactly. But a wicked environment</v>

124
00:15:46.767 --> 00:16:01.094
<v Maya>is like corporate strategy or hiring. You might use an AI to help you filter resumes and hire a marketing director. But you might not know if the AI gave you bad advice for five years until that director runs a campaign that bankrupts the company.</v>

125
00:16:01.214 --> 00:16:14.584
<v Daniel>Precisely. Or take medicine. If you're a doctor getting AI advice on an x-ray, do you actually find out the biopsy results the next day, or does that patient disappear into the broader healthcare system, leaving you with zero feedback on whether the AI was right?</v>

