00:16:14.845 --> 00:16:16.326
<v Maya>Oh, that's dark, but so true.</v>

127
00:16:16.745 --> 00:16:31.272
<v Daniel>So the question for you listening to this right now is, does your real-world workplace actually give you the clean trial-by-trial feedback that a laboratory can? If your workplace is a wicked environment, providing you with AI explanations</v>

128
00:16:31.932 --> 00:16:34.493
<v Daniel>might just be making you dangerously overconfident.</v>

129
00:16:34.866 --> 00:16:49.378
<v Maya>That is a terrifying but necessary reality check. We have to synthesize what this means for all the organizations, the managers, and especially the educators out there who are rushing to deploy AI tools. You cannot just hand your team or your students an AI</v>

130
00:16:49.418 --> 00:16:52.340
<v Maya>assistant, turn it on, and expect magic synergy.</v>

131
00:16:52.501 --> 00:17:06.054
<v Daniel>No, you absolutely cannot. If we connect this to the bigger picture, human learning is the ultimate understudied lever in the AI revolution. We spend billions of dollars training the models and almost zero time training the humans how to interact with them.</v>

132
00:17:06.294 --> 00:17:20.822
<v Maya>So if you are leading a team, you have to deliberately build in feedback loops. You have to offer practice trials where the stakes are zero so people can learn the AI's quirks without crashing the company. And at a higher level, you have to explicitly ask yourself, are we</v>

133
00:17:20.962 --> 00:17:29.927
<v Maya>training our people to rigorously verify the AI or are we just quietly training them to accept whatever it says because it spits out a convincing paragraph?</v>

134
00:17:30.135 --> 00:17:39.557
<v Daniel>Technology is only as good as our ability to adapt to it. The AI penalty we discussed at the start isn't necessarily a failure of the machine. It is a failure of our learning environment.</v>

135
00:17:40.037 --> 00:17:54.739
<v Maya>I want to leave you with a final thought to mull over, something that really builds on this idea of learning environments. The research we looked at today assumes a static AI. It assumes an AI that behaves the exact same way in trial one as it does in trial</v>

136
00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:55.360
<v Maya>100.</v>

137
00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:57.360
<v Daniel>Right, which isn't how modern AI works.</v>

138
00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:07.189
<v Maya>No, because today's generative AI models, the ones we are all using right now on our phones and laptops, they are designed to constantly learn and adapt from our prompts in real time.</v>

139
00:18:07.329 --> 00:18:11.033
<v Daniel>They are moving targets. They change their behavior based on how you interact with them.</v>

140
00:18:11.313 --> 00:18:26.040
<v Maya>Exactly. So if AI is learning and changing its behavior constantly based on you, how can a human ever establish a stable feedback loop to calibrate their trust? If the superhero suit of armor is constantly changing its shape, its weight, and its controls while you are wearing it, you are</v>

